.\" Hand-written. Tests in crates/iron-vault-policy/tests/policy/config_man_page.rs check that it names every key and value the
.\" parser accepts, and that its EXAMPLE section is a valid config. The manual's configuration reference
.\" (book/src/reference/configuration.md) is the full account, with every refusal message.
.TH iron-vault-config 5 "" "Iron Vault" "Iron Vault Manual"
.SH NAME
iron\-vault\-config \- the Iron Vault configuration file
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fI/etc/iron\-vault/config.toml\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
The config says where callers connect (listeners), who each caller is and which routes it may use (callers), and
where each route sends requests and with which credential (routes).
It is one TOML file in UTF\-8, at most 61440 bytes long.
.PP
You never edit the config the service runs.
Write your own copy, anywhere you like, and approve it:
.PP
.RS
\fBsudo iron\-vault config approve\fR \fIFILE\fR
.RE
.PP
The service checks the file, shows its own summary of what changes, and asks you to type \fByes\fR and then the
approval passphrase.
It then signs the config, publishes it as \fIconfig.toml\fR with its signature \fIconfig.toml.sig\fR, and puts it into
force without a restart.
A config that fails a check uses up no approval version, and the active config does not change.
.PP
The signature covers every byte, comments and whitespace included.
If the published copy is edited, the service refuses it: it stops proxying and reports \fBpolicy invalid\fR until you
approve a config again.
.SH GENERAL RULES
\fBUnknown keys are refused\fR in every table, so a misspelt key is an error rather than a setting silently ignored.
.PP
\fBNames\fR of listeners, callers, routes and credentials are 1 to 64 bytes of \fBa\-z\fR, \fB0\-9\fR, \fB_\fR and
\fB\-\fR, starting with a lowercase letter.
.PP
\fBPaths\fR must be absolute, with no \fB..\fR component and no NUL byte.
.PP
The top level holds \fBschema_version\fR and the tables below.
Every table is optional.
A config with only \fBschema_version = 1\fR is valid, and opens no listener.
.TP
\fBschema_version\fR (integer, required)
The version of the file's format.
It must be \fB1\fR in this release.
.SH LISTENERS
A \fB[listeners.\fINAME\fB]\fR table is one socket or address where callers connect.
Each caller names the listener it arrives on.
.TP
\fBunix_socket\fR (path)
A Unix socket, such as \fI/run/iron\-vault/proxy.sock\fR.
Set exactly one of \fBunix_socket\fR and \fBaddress\fR.
.TP
\fBaddress\fR (string)
An IP address and port, such as \fB127.0.0.1:7878\fR or \fB[::1]:7878\fR.
A host name is refused, and so is port 0.
In a protected install, systemd opens the socket and passes it to the service, so each address listener also needs
an \fBiron\-vault\-listener@\fINAME\fB.socket\fR unit with the same address.
.TP
\fBtls_certificate\fR, \fBtls_key\fR (paths)
The listener's TLS certificate chain and private key, in PEM.
Set both or neither.
The certificate must be owned by root or the service account and writable by nobody else; the key must be owned by the
service account, with no group or other permission bits.
They are required on an address outside loopback (\fB127.0.0.0/8\fR and \fB::1\fR), so caller tokens never cross a
network in clear, and refused on a Unix socket.
.TP
\fBtoken_header\fR (string, default \fBProxy\-Authorization\fR)
The header from which callers on this listener send their caller token.
With \fBProxy\-Authorization\fR the caller sends \fBProxy\-Authorization: Bearer\fR \fITOKEN\fR.
With any other header, such as \fBX\-Iron\-Vault\-Token\fR, the whole value is the token.
It must not be a reserved header, apart from \fBProxy\-Authorization\fR itself, nor the slot header of a route this
listener's callers can use.
The service strips it before forwarding.
.SH CALLERS
A \fB[callers.\fINAME\fB]\fR table is one program, or group of programs, that Iron Vault recognises and grants routes
to.
.TP
\fBlistener\fR (listener name, required)
The listener the caller connects to.
.TP
\fBauth\fR (string, default \fBtoken\fR)
How the caller is identified.
All callers on one listener use the same kind.
.RS
.TP
\fBtoken\fR
The caller sends a caller token, whose hash is in \fBtoken_hash\fR.
Anyone who can read the token can use the caller's routes.
.TP
\fBpeer\fR
The caller is the OS account the kernel reports for the connecting process, named in \fBuser\fR.
There is no token to steal.
It needs a \fBunix_socket\fR listener, and every caller on that listener must be a peer caller with a different
account.
.TP
\fBisolated\-network\fR
The caller is recognised by the listener alone.
It must be the only caller on an address listener with TLS, bound to an address only it can reach, such as its own
container network: neither loopback nor a wildcard address.
.RE
.TP
\fBtoken_hash\fR (string, with \fBauth = "token"\fR only)
\fBblake2b:\fR followed by 64 lowercase hex digits, as printed by \fBiron\-vault caller\-token\fR.
The service keeps only the hash.
Token callers on one listener must have different hashes.
To rotate a token, approve a config with the new hash: the old token stops working at that moment.
.TP
\fBuser\fR (account name, with \fBauth = "peer"\fR only)
The account: \fBa\-z\fR or \fB_\fR, then \fBa\-z\fR, \fB0\-9\fR, \fB_\fR or \fB\-\fR, with an optional trailing
\fB$\fR, at most 32 bytes.
The account must exist on the host, and must not be root, the service account or the overflow uid
(\fBnobody\fR).
.TP
\fBroutes\fR (array of route names, required)
The routes the caller may use.
It must not be empty or repeat a route, and every route it names must be defined.
.TP
\fBrate_limits\fR (table)
A rate limit for this caller on one of its routes, keyed by route name, such as
\fB[callers.code.rate_limits]\fR with \fBgithub = { requests = 30, per_seconds = 60, burst = 5 }\fR.
It applies on top of the route's own limit, and may be stricter but never looser.
.SH ROUTES
A \fB[routes.\fINAME\fB]\fR table maps requests under a path prefix to one upstream, with one credential in one slot.
.TP
\fBprefix\fR (string, required)
The path under which callers send requests for this route.
The service takes it off and sends the rest of the path upstream.
It matches whole segments: \fB/fx\fR matches \fB/fx\fR and \fB/fx/latest\fR, never \fB/fxx\fR.
It must not be \fB/\fR, contain a wildcard or \fB%\fR, end in a slash, or overlap another route's prefix.
.TP
\fBupstream\fR (string, required)
The HTTPS URL that requests are sent to, with an optional base path, such as
\fBhttps://api.venice.ai/api/v1\fR.
It starts with \fBhttps://\fR in lowercase, and has no user\-info, query or fragment.
An IP address host must be in canonical form.
A trailing slash is dropped, and the port defaults to 443.
.TP
\fBcredential\fR (credential name, required)
A credential in the vault, stored with \fBiron\-vault add\fR.
The origins given to \fBadd\fR are kept inside the encrypted record, and the route's upstream must be one of them, so
a config change can never send an existing key to a new host.
Only \fBiron\-vault replace\fR changes them.
.TP
\fBslot\fR (table or string, required)
The one place the service writes the credential.
It first removes whatever the caller sent there, so a placeholder key is replaced, never forwarded.
.RS
.TP
\fB{ header = "\fINAME\fB" }\fR
The header holds the key alone.
\fBheader\fR must not be a reserved header; \fBAuthorization\fR is allowed.
.TP
\fB{ header = "\fINAME\fB", format = "Bearer {}" }\fR
The key goes in place of \fB{}\fR.
\fBformat\fR is printable ASCII with exactly one \fB{}\fR and no other braces, and defaults to \fB{}\fR.
.TP
\fB{ query = "\fINAME\fB" }\fR
The key is added to the query as \fINAME\fB=\fIKEY\fR.
The name is made only of \fBA\-Z a\-z 0\-9 \- . _ ~\fR.
.TP
\fB"basic"\fR
For a credential added with a username and password: \fBAuthorization: Basic\fR and the encoded pair.
.RE
.TP
\fBmethods\fR (array of strings, required)
The methods the route allows: any of \fBGET\fR, \fBHEAD\fR, \fBPOST\fR, \fBPUT\fR, \fBPATCH\fR, \fBDELETE\fR and
\fBOPTIONS\fR, in upper case, without repeats.
\fBCONNECT\fR and \fBTRACE\fR are never allowed.
.TP
\fBpaths\fR (array of strings, required)
The path patterns the route allows.
They match the request path after the route prefix, as the provider documents it, without the upstream's base path
(the service adds that when it forwards the request), and never see the query.
\fB*\fR matches exactly one segment and \fB**\fR zero or more; a wildcard must be a whole segment.
\fB["/**"]\fR allows every path, and must be written out: an empty list is refused.
A pattern starts with \fB/\fR, has no empty or dot segment and no trailing slash.
Literal segments hold letters, digits and \fB\- . _ ~ ! $ & \(aq ( ) + , ; = : @\fR.
.TP
\fBrate_limit\fR (table, required)
\fB{ requests =\fR \fIN\fB, per_seconds =\fR \fIN\fB, burst =\fR \fIN\fB }\fR, three nonzero whole numbers.
A token bucket that holds at most \fBburst\fR tokens and refills at \fBrequests\fR tokens every \fBper_seconds\fR
seconds.
Each request takes a token, and a request that finds the bucket empty gets \fB429\fR.
All the route's callers share the bucket.
Every bucket starts empty each time the service becomes Ready, such as after \fBunlock\fR or an approval.
.TP
\fBconfirm\fR (string, default \fBnone\fR)
\fBnone\fR, the only value this release accepts.
\fBtouch\fR is named for a later release; no touch confirmation provider exists in this one, so a config with
\fBtouch\fR is refused when it is read, before any approval prompt.
.TP
\fBca_file\fR (path)
A PEM file of CA certificates that the route trusts instead of the built\-in roots.
Install it where only an administrator can write.
If it cannot be read, the config is still approved, but the routes that pin it are unavailable.
.SH RESERVED HEADERS
The service removes these from every request before forwarding, and sets \fBHost\fR itself.
None may be a slot, and none but \fBProxy\-Authorization\fR may be a \fBtoken_header\fR.
Names are compared without regard to case.
.PP
\fBConnection\fR, \fBKeep\-Alive\fR, \fBProxy\-Connection\fR, \fBProxy\-Authenticate\fR,
\fBProxy\-Authorization\fR, \fBTE\fR, \fBTrailer\fR, \fBTransfer\-Encoding\fR, \fBUpgrade\fR, \fBHost\fR,
\fBContent\-Length\fR, \fBForwarded\fR, \fBVia\fR, \fBX\-Real\-IP\fR, \fBTrue\-Client\-IP\fR, \fBX\-Client\-IP\fR,
\fBCF\-Connecting\-IP\fR, \fBX\-Host\fR, \fBX\-Original\-Host\fR, \fBX\-HTTP\-Host\-Override\fR,
\fBX\-HTTP\-Method\-Override\fR, \fBX\-Method\-Override\fR, \fBX\-HTTP\-Method\fR, \fBX\-Original\-URL\fR,
\fBX\-Rewrite\-URL\fR, and every header starting with \fBX\-Forwarded\-\fR.
.SH LIMITS
The \fB[limits]\fR table holds service\-wide limits.
Every key is optional, and a key left out keeps its default.
Zero is refused, and so is a value above the ceiling.
.TP
\fBmax_header_bytes\fR (default 65536, ceiling 1048576)
A request line and headers, in bytes.
.TP
\fBmax_header_count\fR (default 100, ceiling 1000)
A request's headers, in number.
.TP
\fBmax_request_body_bytes\fR (default 10485760, ceiling 1073741824)
A request body, in bytes.
.TP
\fBmax_decompressed_response_bytes\fR (default 67108864, ceiling 4294967296)
A response body after decompression for scrubbing, in bytes.
.TP
\fBmax_decompression_ratio\fR (default 100, ceiling 10000)
Decompressed size over compressed size.
.TP
\fBrequest_timeout_secs\fR (default 300, ceiling 3600)
A whole request, in seconds.
.TP
\fBupstream_timeout_secs\fR (default 300, ceiling 600)
Waiting for the upstream's response headers, in seconds.
It must not exceed \fBrequest_timeout_secs\fR.
.TP
\fBmax_connections\fR (default 256, ceiling 65536)
Concurrent caller connections.
.SH LOGGING
The \fB[logging]\fR table says where the audit log goes.
.TP
\fBdestination\fR (string)
Where the log goes: \fBstdout\fR, or an absolute directory in which the service writes \fIiron\-vault.log\fR.
When it is not set, the log goes to \fI/var/log/iron\-vault\fR in a protected install.
A directory must exist before you approve and lie outside the state and config directories.
The service account must own it, with no group write and no access for others; \fBconfig approve\fR checks it.
.SH RECOVERY
The \fB[recovery]\fR table sets how often the service reminds you to check the offline recovery key.
.TP
\fBreminder_days\fR (integer, default 90)
How many days may pass after the last \fBiron\-vault recovery check\fR before the service reminds you to run
another: a whole number from 7 to 365.
.SH EXAMPLE
One socket for two local accounts, each recognised by the kernel, and a loopback address for a tool that can only
take an HTTP URL, with a caller token:
.PP
.EX
schema_version = 1

[listeners.local]
unix_socket = "/run/iron\-vault/proxy.sock"

[listeners.loopback]
address = "127.0.0.1:7878"

[callers.code]
listener = "local"
auth = "peer"
user = "code"
routes = ["github"]

[callers.trading]
listener = "local"
auth = "peer"
user = "trading"
routes = ["coingecko"]

[callers.brave\-cli]
listener = "loopback"
token_hash = "blake2b:0123456789abcdef00112233445566778899aabbccddeeff0f1e2d3c4b5a6978"
routes = ["brave"]

[routes.github]
prefix = "/github"
upstream = "https://api.github.com"
credential = "github"
slot = { header = "Authorization", format = "Bearer {}" }
methods = ["GET", "POST"]
paths = ["/user/repos", "/repos/*/*/issues"]
rate_limit = { requests = 60, per_seconds = 60, burst = 10 }

[routes.coingecko]
prefix = "/coingecko"
upstream = "https://api.coingecko.com"
credential = "coingecko"
slot = { header = "x\-cg\-demo\-api\-key" }
methods = ["GET"]
paths = ["/api/v3/**"]
rate_limit = { requests = 30, per_seconds = 60, burst = 5 }

[routes.brave]
prefix = "/brave"
upstream = "https://api.search.brave.com"
credential = "brave"
slot = { header = "X\-Subscription\-Token" }
methods = ["GET"]
paths = ["/res/v1/web/search"]
rate_limit = { requests = 60, per_seconds = 60, burst = 10 }
.EE
.PP
The \fBcode\fR account then reaches GitHub with:
.PP
.RS
\fBcurl \-\-unix\-socket /run/iron\-vault/proxy.sock http://localhost/github/user/repos\fR
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI/etc/iron\-vault/config.toml\fR
The approved config, as the service published it.
.TP
\fI/etc/iron\-vault/config.toml.sig\fR
Its approval signature.
.TP
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/iron\-vault\-dev/config.toml\fR
The approved config in development mode.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBiron\-vault\fR(1), \fBiron\-vault\-config\-approve\fR(1), \fBiron\-vault\-caller\-token\fR(1),
\fBiron\-vault\-add\fR(1), \fBiron\-vault\-replace\fR(1)
.PP
The configuration reference in the Iron Vault manual lists every rule with the refusal it gives.
